body:
I think this is what you're looking for:

```
    TranslationResult result;

    // TODO: Correct this check when we figure out what MISA can do
    if (CSR[misa].S == 1) {
      result = translate(virtual_address, MemoryOperation:Fetch, mode(), encoding);
    } else {
      result.paddr = virtual_address;
    }

    # may raise an exception
    access_check(result.paddr, xlen(), $pc, MemoryOperation::Fetch, ExceptionCode::InstructionAccessFault, mode());

    if (xlen() == 32) {
      addr = read_physical_memory<32>(result.paddr);
    } else {
      addr = read_physical_memory<64>(result.paddr);
    }
```

ahh, I see. For clarity, then, it's probably best to pull those checks into a function that is called from jvt.sw_read, cm.jalt, and cm.jt.

That function can go in any file reachable (directly or via includes) from `spec/std/ids/isa/globals.isa` (including a new file you create, if you want).

That function will look like this:

```
function check_zcmt_enabled {
  arguments
    Bits<INSTR_ENC_SIZE> encoding  # needed because $encoding is only in-scope in operation()
  description { 
    ...
  }
  body {
    # from jvt.sw_read
  }
}
```

and call it:

```
check_zcmt_enabled($encoding);
```

also, looking at what's in zcmt.sw_read, this looks suspicious (should be IllegalInstruction?):

```
  if ((mode() != PrivilegeMode::M) && implemented?(ExtensionName::Smstateen)) {
    if (CSR[mstateen0].JVT == 1'b0) {
      raise(ExceptionCode::VirtualInstruction, mode(), $encoding);
    }
  }
```

> What happens when you try to pass directly to direct_csr_lookup?

This works:
```
  Csr csr_handle = direct_csr_lookup(0x017);
  XReg jvt = csr_sw_read(csr_handle);
```
